// Exercise 5 -- the contract is the specification, and it is yours.
//
// The brief (exercises/05-contract/brief.md) says what `count_words` is
// for. Below is the contract a first draft usually has: two examples.
// Two examples are a *hint*, not a contract, and the point of this
// exercise is to find out how much of the gap an agent will fill with a
// guess. Add the tests that make a wrong answer impossible -- BEFORE you
// show it to the agent.
//
//   cancho satisfy work/05-contract/count_words.contract.cho work/05-contract/candidate.cho --std
//
// A contract has no `main`; a candidate lives in another file, and
// `satisfy` runs this file's tests against it. The two are checked as
// ONE program, so an import in both under the same name is refused --
// which is why a contract imports `as` a name of its own (`t` below)
// and never fights the candidate for `test` or `result`.
import std.test as t;

fn test_two_words() -> [] int {
    return t.assert_eq(count_words("hello world"), 2);
}

fn test_one_word() -> [] int {
    return t.assert_eq(count_words("one"), 1);
}
